# Evidence construction and limits

Host attempt05 is authoritative. Attempts01–04 are preserved as failed historical
work with their exact reports, source snapshots and logs. The passing module
contains the explicit cast required by the actual public read-only getter whose
pinned declaration lacks const; both original and corrected bytes are retained.
Host types, crypto callbacks, RTOS locks, capacity inputs and adaptive-input seam
remain explicit limitations. Whole canonical reclaim code executes on that host,
but host capacity inputs are not target ABI or physical memory evidence.

The24 configure-only projects execute actual CMake with no compiler/SDK export.
Prep02 separately freezes the later supporting-source guard/helper changes and
the same corrected module. Its manifest/readiness and22 guard cases are preserved
as preparation evidence, followed by the completed actual target report
`@@TARGET_SHA@@`. No live/partial report is accepted as final.

Each of ten actual target variants has its own frozen manifest, SDK build/config,
ELF, runtime bundle and exact oracle. Four option combinations are independently
linked. SDK commit is `7101770dc6db2667b3c477cc31365dd1acd6db4e`. The target
assertion profile remains enabled, two cores execute, and QEMU has no NIC.
Generic panic, abort, corruption, timeout or unrelated failure never counts as
semantic-control success. Real backend NULL observations and injected rollback
are recorded separately; no handshake, network or standards claim follows.

The original17 raw SDK source/header identities and normalized guard identities
are retained together without normalizing archive payload bytes. Actual compiled
header closure, compiler flags, allocator pointer words and SDK ownership-call
disassembly accompany the execution bundles. ELF words are derived metadata tied
to an omitted original ELF SHA, not a replacement for that binary. Content maps
retain original paths and historical versions. Tool executables/ROMs and target
or host binaries are omitted explicitly; reported Linux tool sizes stay unknown
because packaging does not launch WSL or tools.

The offline verifier needs only Python's standard library. Packaging invokes no
SDK/QEMU/WSL/Git or network/device process. It neither changes source/SDK nor
qualifies a production application. Integration with radio service ownership,
cooperative cancellation, full firmware profiles, physical behavior and measured
benefit remain future gates. AAC, Ogg and deferred FLAC are excluded.
